     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-11-04 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bandhavgarh Tiger Reserve Reintroduced Gaur Population Surpasses 220</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#environment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Decades after disappearing from Madhya Pradesh's Bandhavgarh Tiger Reserve, the gaur, or Indian bison, made a successful comeback following conservation efforts. In 2011-12, a herd of 40 gaur was reintroduced from Kanha Tiger Reserve, and the population now stands at over 220. Recognized as the world's largest wild bovine, the gaur is categorized as vulnerable on the IUCN Red List of Species and holds the highest legal protection in India under Schedule I of the Wildlife Protection Act, 1972. Habitat fragmentation and loss pose ongoing threats to the species across its range.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/bandhavgarh-tiger-reserve-reintroduced-gaur-population-surpa.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate, concise, captures all key figures and context from the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://india.mongabay.com/short-article/2026/11/the-worlds-largest-bovine/" rel="noopener">Mongabay India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
