     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-11-04 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Biocon Q2 Profit Halves to ₹41 Crore Amid Delayed Shipments</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Biocon</span> <span>#Pharmaceuticals</span> <span>#Q2 Results</span> <span>#Earnings</span></div>
       <p>Bengaluru-based pharmaceutical major Biocon reported a significant 52 percent year-on-year (Y-o-Y) decline in consolidated net profit, falling to ₹41 crore for the second quarter of FY27 (Q2FY27). This reduction was primarily attributed to approximately $20 million worth of shipments missing the quarter-end cut-off, while their associated costs had already been booked. Despite the profit dip, revenue from operations saw a modest 2 percent growth, reaching ₹4,389 crore.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/biocon-q2-profit-halves-to-41-crore-amid-delayed-shipments.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The Q2 net profit of ₹41 crore is a notable decrease from ₹85 crore recorded a year earlier and ₹141 crore in the preceding June quarter. Following the announcement of these results during market hours, Biocon shares closed up 0.19 percent at ₹344 on the BSE. Shreehas Tambe, Managing Director of Biocon, clarified that the delayed shipments, representing roughly 5 percent of biopharma revenue, were impacted by ongoing geopolitical disruptions and only reached customers after September.</p><p>Tambe explained that accounting rules prevented the recognition of this revenue in Q2, even though the costs were incurred. He confirmed that these products were sold in October, and their revenue will be reflected in Q3. Tambe reassured stakeholders, stating, &quot;There is no underlying business challenge,&quot; and characterized the situation as purely a &quot;revenue recognition issue.&quot; Earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation, and amortisation (Ebitda) for Q2 fell 7 percent to ₹865 crore, with the margin slipping to 20 percent from 22 percent. Tambe expects margins to remain in the mid-20s range but did not provide specific guidance for the second half of the fiscal year.</p><p>Biopharma revenue increased 5 percent to ₹3,583 crore, driven by biosimilars growing 3 percent and generics up 10 percent. Services revenue, primarily from Syngene, experienced a 9 percent Y-o-Y decline but grew 13 percent sequentially. For the first half of the fiscal year, biopharma revenue rose 11 percent, and net profit increased 57 percent to ₹182 crore. Tambe anticipates a stronger second half, citing four key growth drivers.</p><p>These drivers include the ustekinumab biosimilar Yesintek, which holds a 34 percent share of the US market, and the aflibercept biosimilar Yesafili, which is currently ramping up. Additionally, Biocon has secured a 10-year pertuzumab supply deal in Brazil, valued at approximately $400 million, with supplies commencing in Q4. A European pertuzumab launch is also expected in Q4, targeting a $600-700 million market where Biocon and Organon are the only approved players. Furthermore, a second insulin fill-finish line in Malaysia has become operational, expanding capacity to meet US demand. Regarding semaglutide, Biocon has tech-transferred its product to Brazilian partner Biomm, which is seeking approval from regulator Anvisa, a process Tambe estimated could take 12 to 24 months. He declined to comment on the regulatory status in Canada. Addressing the US midterm elections, Tambe noted that both political parties focus on reducing healthcare costs, and Biocon expects to remain insulated, asserting, &quot;We are a part of the solution.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: Biocon's Q3 results for revenue recognition and the ramp-up of new product launches and deals.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent, detailed coverage of the financial results and executive statements.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/companies/quarterly-results/biocon-q2-profit-halves-to-41-crore-as-delayed-shipments-weigh-on-margins-126110401735_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
