       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · Times of India · 2026-11-04 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>British Columbia Court Upholds C$5.5M Charitable Bequest Over Photocopied Will</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>The Supreme Court of British Columbia has ruled in favor of Shirley Isobel Alexander’s original estate plan, directing her C$5.5 million fortune to registered charities. Alexander, who died in April 2025 without a spouse or children, had established a clear legal framework for her assets through a 2018 will and a 2025 codicil.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/british-columbia-court-upholds-c55m-charitable-bequest-over-.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>A friend of the deceased had challenged these documents, presenting photocopies of an alleged 2024 will that named her as the sole beneficiary. The court rejected the claim after the woman failed to produce original documents or credible witnesses, upholding the charitable intentions documented in Alexander’s verified legal records.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate summary of the court case and the rejection of the disputed photocopied documents.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/world/us/an-80-year-old-british-columbia-woman-left-a-c5-5-million-estate-to-charity-but-after-she-died-a-friend-produced-photocopies-of-a-will-naming-herself-sole-heir-on-october-29-a-court-ruled-the-charities-get-it-all/articleshow/135522672.cms" rel="noopener">Times of India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
