     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-04 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Endangered Bogong Moths Blanket Australian Cities During Annual Migration</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#science</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Millions of bogong moths have blanketed cities across Australia, including homes and cars, during their annual migration from southern breeding grounds to the cool caves of the Australian Alps. Despite the surge in urban sightings, the International Union for Conservation of Nature still lists the species as endangered due to climate change heating up their mountain boulderfields.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/endangered-bogong-moths-blanket-australian-cities-during-ann.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Scientists remain unsure whether the population is rebuilding or if favorable breeding conditions, food availability, and strong winds are driving more moths into cities. Citizen scientists are utilizing platforms like iNaturalist to document hundreds of sightings, revealing a strong preference for bottlebrushes and spider flowers while helping bridge crucial gaps in insect biology research.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately reports the moth migration, endangered status, citizen science data and preferences without adding unsupported details.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-millions-bogong-moths-blanketing-cities.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
