     <article class="story single" data-beat="environment">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">ENVIRONMENT</span> · Mongabay India · 2026-11-04 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Extreme Humid Heat Sparks Health Concerns and Work-Hour Shifts in Assam</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#environment</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Outdoor workers in Assam's tea gardens and agricultural fields are experiencing severe fatigue and health toll due to high nighttime temperatures, forcing shifts in working hours to start at 5 AM. According to a new study in the Journal of Geophysical Research Atmospheres, India accounts for over half of the global rise in potential productivity loss from extreme humid heat since the mid-1980s. Continued exposure can lead to severe medical conditions like heat stroke and death. However, the study notes that humid heat stress is closely linked to the El Niño-Southern Oscillation, offering potential early warning signs.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/extreme-humid-heat-sparks-health-concerns-and-work-hour-shif.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Captures the core narrative, study findings, and local impacts tightly within limits.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
