       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">DEFENCE</span> · Defence (aggregate) · 2026-11-04 · editor 8/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Government Grants Two-Year Extension to Unselected Agniveers</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Agnipath</span> <span>#Defence</span> <span>#Government Policy</span> <span>#Agniveers</span></div>
       <p>The Indian government has introduced a significant modification to the Agnipath scheme, granting a two-year service extension to Agniveers not selected for permanent roles. This &quot;big change,&quot; as reported by ThePrint, specifically applies to the 75% of recruits who do not transition into regular service after their initial four-year term.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/government-grants-two-year-extension-to-unselected-agniveers.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>This extension aims to provide additional opportunities and continuity for these personnel, marking a notable adjustment to the Agnipath recruitment model. The scheme initially envisioned a short-term engagement for a majority of its recruits, with a smaller percentage being inducted into permanent service.</p><p>What to watch: The impact of this extension on the overall Agnipath framework and future military recruitment strategies.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurately captures the essence of the short source text without adding unverified claims.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://news.google.?oc=5" rel="noopener">Defence (aggregate)</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
