     <article class="story single" data-beat="national">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">NATIONAL</span> · The Hindu · 2026-11-04 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span><span class="badge quick">quick bite</span></div>
       <h1>Government Urologists Assigned as Casualty Officers in Tamil Nadu Due to Lack of Vacancies</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#national</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Twelve government urologists who completed their M.Ch. in 2025 have spent nearly a year working as casualty medical officers in emergency departments across Tamil Nadu. Sources indicate that no new posts have been created in urology departments over the years, leaving several government medical college hospitals with only a single urologist. Furthermore, facilities in Karur, Sivagangai, Tiruppur, Tiruvannamalai, Ariyalur, and Kallakurichi currently operate without any urology services. Consequently, these super-speciality graduates have been assigned to handle emergency care and accident registry entries.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/government-urologists-assigned-as-casualty-officers-in-tamil.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async">
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate, concise, and captures all essential facts from the brief source cleanly.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/national/tamil-nadu/urologists-work-as-casualty-medical-officers-in-emergency-depts-owing-to-lack-of-appropriate-vacancies-across-tn/article71636838.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
