     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-04 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>KM3NeT Collaboration Tests Einstein's Laws, Sets Limits on Lorentz Invariance Violation</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Science</span> <span>#Physics</span> <span>#Neutrinos</span> <span>#KM3NeT</span></div>
       <p>Researchers from the international KM3NeT collaboration have analyzed 5,828 atmospheric neutrino candidate events detected by KM3NeT/ORCA, a neutrino telescope situated deep in the Mediterranean Sea, to investigate the fundamental principle of Lorentz invariance. This principle, central to Einstein's theory of relativity, posits that the laws of physics are uniform across the universe. However, some theories attempting to unify gravity with quantum physics suggest that spacetime might be composed of discrete units, potentially leading to violations of Lorentz invariance. The study, published in the Journal of Cosmology and Astroparticle Physics, found no direct evidence of such a violation, but successfully established new experimental limits on the possible magnitude of these effects.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/km3net-collaboration-tests-einsteins-laws-sets-limits-on-lor.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The team also provided the first direct constraints on potential differences in the behavior of muon and tau neutrinos. Alba Domi, a researcher at Erlangen Centre for Astroparticle Physics (ECAP) and a corresponding author, explained that probing Lorentz invariance violations offers a method to explore whether spacetime is continuous or discrete. Neutrinos are particularly valuable for these investigations due to their minimal interaction with matter, allowing them to traverse vast distances, and their unique property of oscillation, where they transform between flavors. Discrepancies between predicted and observed oscillation behavior could indicate new physics.</p><p>The analysis involved 1.4 years of data collected from the initial six detection units of KM3NeT/ORCA, comparing observed neutrino energies and arrival directions against theoretical predictions. Despite the detector being in an early, partial configuration, the results are competitive with those from established experiments like IceCube and Super-Kamiokande. Lukas Hennig, also from ECAP and a corresponding author, noted the potential of KM3NeT/ORCA, even with a smaller detector, to test fundamental physics principles. The detector is still undergoing expansion towards its full planned configuration, promising more sensitive searches for subtle effects in the future.</p><p>Future investigations aim to extend to KM3NeT/ARCA, which focuses on higher-energy neutrinos from astrophysical sources. These neutrinos are considered especially promising, as some theorized effects of Lorentz invariance violation would become more pronounced over longer distances and at elevated energies. Domi indicated that ARCA could be well-suited for this research, pending the accumulation of sufficient astrophysical neutrino events. The significance of high-energy neutrino research was underscored by the 2026 Nobel Prize in Physics, awarded to Francis Halzen for his contributions to the IceCube Neutrino Observatory.</p><p>What to watch: The continued expansion of KM3NeT/ORCA and future analyses using KM3NeT/ARCA for higher-energy neutrinos.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Accurate, detailed science reporting capturing key stats and quotes from the text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-einstein-laws-bottom-mediterranean-sea.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
