     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-11-04 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Maaya Revathi Makes History at Chennai Open, First Indian Woman Since Sania Mirza in WTA Quarterfinals</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tennis</span> <span>#WTA Tour</span> <span>#Maaya Rajeshwaran Revathi</span> <span>#Sania Mirza</span></div>
       <p>India's teenage sensation Maaya Rajeshwaran Revathi scripted a significant chapter in the country's tennis history on Wednesday, becoming the first Indian woman since Sania Mirza in 2012 to reach a WTA Tour-level singles quarterfinal. The 17-year-old Maaya, currently ranked 669th in the world, achieved this milestone at the Chennai Open.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/maaya-revathi-makes-history-at-chennai-open-first-indian-wom.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Maaya secured a straight-set 6-4 6-3 victory over Polish qualifier Weronika Falkowska, ranked 277th, in the second round. This win is particularly noteworthy as no Indian player had advanced beyond the second round in the previous two editions of the Chennai Open. Her achievement ends an almost 15-year wait for an Indian woman to reach the last eight of a WTA Tour singles event, a feat last accomplished by Sania Mirza at the Pattaya Open in February 2012.</p><p>This breakthrough in Chennai marks the latest step in Maaya's rapid ascent on the professional circuit this season. She announced her arrival in October by winning a maiden W35 singles title in Lagos, Portugal, as a qualifier without dropping a set. Furthermore, she also claimed the doubles title in Lagos alongside Ukraine's Yelyzaveta Kotliar, making her the youngest Indian to win a professional singles title since Sania Mirza. Maaya recently relocated from the Rafa Nadal Academy in Spain to the Elite Tennis Center in southern France.</p><p>The match against Falkowska was far from straightforward, characterized by a succession of breaks, double faults, and unforced errors from both players. However, Maaya's attacking instincts and ability to seize crucial moments ultimately proved decisive. She began the first set erratically, opening with a double fault and dropping her serve, but immediately broke back as Falkowska struggled with returns. The opening set saw five consecutive breaks of serve before Falkowska finally held for a 4-2 lead. Maaya responded by breaking back and holding serve to take a 5-4 advantage, eventually converting her second set point after Falkowska's errors.</p><p>The second set followed a similar pattern, with Falkowska attempting to hit with greater weight and Maaya seeking to impose herself through aggressive shot-making. Maaya, however, found greater consistency when it mattered most, breaking Falkowska in the eighth game to establish a 5-3 lead. She then successfully served out the match on her third match point, securing her historic spot in the quarterfinals.</p><p>What to watch: Maaya Revathi's performance in the upcoming quarterfinal match at the Chennai Open.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively incorporates all key details, including the match progression, historical context, and the player's recent career moves.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://sports.ndtv.com/tennis/maaya-revathi-makes-history-becomes-first-indian-since-sania-mirza-to-reach-wta-tour-singles-last-eight-12284676#publisher=newsstand" rel="noopener">NDTV Sports</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
