     <article class="story single" data-beat="finance">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">FINANCE</span> · BS Personal Finance · 2026-11-04 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Markets Gain on US Midterm Results, Nifty Up 5.6% in Five Sessions</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#stock market</span> <span>#US elections</span> <span>#Nifty</span> <span>#Sensex</span></div>
       <p>Indian benchmark indices saw extended gains on Wednesday, reacting to the Democrats' sweep in the US midterm elections. The Nifty 50 traded 0.63 percent higher at its day's high of 23,960.60, marking a 5.6 percent rise over five sessions and reaching a two-month high. The Sensex also closed up by 0.5 percent, with only 88 Nifty 500 stocks declining on Wednesday. This broad market rally saw the total market value of listed companies increase by approximately ₹6.4 trillion.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/markets-gain-on-us-midterm-results-nifty-up-56-in-five-sessi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Sector-wise, Nifty Media emerged as the best-performing index, rising 5.15 percent to a new 52-week high. Within this, Saregama jumped 13.3 percent after its results, and Zee Entertainment rose 10.1 percent. Nifty Realty gained 2.85 percent and Nifty Metal saw a 2.80 percent increase. Conversely, the IT index declined 1.5 percent, potentially as the &quot;AI hedge&quot; lost some appeal, according to Siddhartha Khemka, head of research at Motilal Oswal Financial Services. Foreign portfolio investors (FPIs) invested ₹7,284 crore on Wednesday, the highest since October 28, 2025, while domestic institutional investors (DIIs) offloaded ₹3,712 crore.</p><p>The India rally was also supported by Brent crude oil prices staying near $100 per barrel, though this is &quot;low&quot; compared to its six-month high near $118 last week. Brent fell over 7 percent on Monday on hopes of a US-Iran deal, trading around $102.50 on Wednesday afternoon, despite reports of ongoing tensions in West Asia. The rupee strengthened to about 94.6 per dollar, a two-week high, although still 2.4 percent above May's record low of 96.96. The 10-year gilt yield also fell for a third straight session to about 7.26 percent. Ambareesh Baliga, an independent market analyst, attributed the market's optimism to a potential end to the West Asia crisis following the US election outcome, alongside strong Q2 earnings and healthy corrections in the IT sector.</p><p>For ordinary savers and taxpayers, these market movements indicate a period of increased investor confidence, particularly in specific sectors like media, realty, and metals. The flow of foreign capital into Indian equities suggests global interest, while the rupee's relative strength offers some stability. However, the decline in IT stocks and the volatile situation in West Asia, which influences crude oil prices, highlight areas of ongoing uncertainty. The fall in gilt yields could also affect bond investors.</p><p>What to watch: The US administration's next actions on West Asia and global crude oil prices.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Captures financial metrics, sector movements, and expert commentary faithfully.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/markets/news/markets-extend-gains-on-us-midterm-results-as-democrats-sweep-elections-126110401953_1.html" rel="noopener">BS Personal Finance</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
