     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Business Standard · 2026-11-04 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>NCERT Introduces Personal Finance and Entrepreneurship Chapters in Class 9 Textbooks</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Education</span> <span>#NCERT</span> <span>#Personal Finance</span> <span>#Entrepreneurship</span></div>
       <p>The National Council for Educational Research and Training (NCERT) has integrated dedicated chapters on personal finance and entrepreneurship into the Economics section of its new Class 9 Social Science textbook. Titled 'Managing Your Personal Finances' and 'From Ideas to Startups,' these additions aim to equip students with practical skills in money management and business development.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/ncert-introduces-personal-finance-and-entrepreneurship-chapt.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The personal finance chapter covers five core areas: income, budgeting, saving, investing, and protection and risk management. Students will learn about investment options such as fixed deposits, government savings schemes, bonds, stocks, and mutual funds, alongside concepts like inflation, compounding, and risk-return relationships. It also delves into insurance, borrowing, credit, and equated monthly instalments (EMIs). A section on taxation explains direct and indirect taxes and introduces the income-tax slab system under the new tax regime, including worked examples for calculating tax liability.</p><p>The entrepreneurship chapter uses examples from Indian businesses to illustrate business development. It covers business plans, the various functions involved in running an enterprise, and the basics of profit-and-loss accounts. Furthermore, it introduces students to India's startup ecosystem and highlights the crucial role of micro, small, and medium enterprises (MSMEs) in employment generation and economic growth.</p><p>These curriculum additions are part of a broader overhaul under the National Education Policy (NEP) 2020 and the National Curriculum Framework for School Education (NCF-SE) 2023, which also incorporate emerging areas like artificial intelligence (AI) and computational thinking (CT). Sanjeev Sanyal, a member of the Economic Advisory Council to the Prime Minister, highlighted these additions on Wednesday via a post on X, noting that the initiative provides &quot;useful life skills&quot; and positively portrays risk-taking, innovation, and business, a departure from previous textbook portrayals of &quot;hoarding&quot; and &quot;black-markets.&quot; The move aligns with growing retail investor participation in the stock market and increased SIP collections by mutual funds, which rose to ₹32,490 crore in September 2026.</p><p>What to watch: The long-term impact of these practical skills on students' financial literacy and entrepreneurial spirit.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Thorough summary of the new NCERT Class 9 textbook additions regarding personal finance and entrepreneurship.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.business-standard.com/education/news/ncert-adds-personal-finance-entrepreneurship-chapters-to-class-9-textbook-126110401803_1.html" rel="noopener">Business Standard</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
