     <article class="story single" data-beat="sports">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SPORTS</span> · NDTV Sports · 2026-11-04 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>New Zealand Pacer Praises Virat Kohli's Masterclass in ODI Victory</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#ODI</span> <span>#Virat Kohli</span> <span>#Jacob Duffy</span></div>
       <p>Star batter Virat Kohli's imperious knock of 74 runs off just 51 balls on Wednesday was instrumental in setting up India's five-wicket victory over New Zealand in the first One-Day International. India successfully chased a revised target of 143 with 56 balls remaining in the rain-curtailed match. Awestruck Kiwi pacer Jacob Duffy, who was Kohli's teammate in the title-winning Royal Challengers Bengaluru side in IPL 2026, remarked that there &quot;was no hole&quot; in the maestro's game during the post-match press conference.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/new-zealand-pacer-praises-virat-kohlis-masterclass-in-odi-vi.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Duffy, who has faced Kohli's batting prowess in the RCB nets, joked about the quick chase, stating, &quot;Yeah, it was a bit annoying, wasn't it?&quot; He elaborated that on small targets, a player with Kohli's intent can easily break the chase. Duffy praised Kohli's ability to handle bounce, get inside hook and pull shots, and hit the ball down the ground, adding, &quot;No surprise, he's a good player. He's done it for a lot of years, and he did that to me in the nets a lot in Bangalore.&quot;</p><p>The 32-year-old pacer, however, pondered whether Kohli would adopt the same aggressive approach if India were setting a target or facing a larger score. He hinted at intel gathered during their time in Bangalore nets but ultimately concluded, &quot;there are not many holes in his game, to be fair.&quot; Duffy noted Kohli's effective use of pace on small grounds and his capability to hit over the bowler's head.</p><p>Duffy also lauded the performances of India's bowlers, specifically Gurnoor Brar, who took 3 wickets for 44 runs, and Arshdeep Singh, with 4 wickets for 70 runs. Brar, standing 6-foot-5-inch tall, repaid captain Shubman Gill's faith by intimidating New Zealand batters with his speed and bounce, helping dismiss the Black Caps for a below-par 185 in 40.2 overs. Duffy conceded that &quot;180 at Eden Park is never going to be enough,&quot; acknowledging that the Indian bowlers used the bounce of the surface effectively, while New Zealand's batting wasn't at its best initially.</p><p>Despite New Zealand reducing India to 136/5 in the 22nd over, Duffy felt it was &quot;a little bit too little, too late.&quot; He affirmed New Zealand's intent for the series, stating, &quot;We're not going to back down, we're going to come hard at these guys.&quot; Duffy also discussed the challenges of defending targets in specific conditions, noting that rain added moisture, causing the ball to skid and removing the tackiness. He expressed confidence that the team would &quot;adapt and adjust&quot; for future games, believing such a batting performance &quot;isn't going to happen again like that.&quot;</p><p>What to watch: New Zealand's adaptation in the upcoming matches against India.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Captures all facts, quotes, and statistics from the detailed cricket report accurately.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
