     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-11-04 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Pangram Expands AI Detection to Images and Video</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#tech</span> <span>#brief</span></div>
       <p>Pangram, a provider of AI text-detection tools, has launched new capabilities to identify AI-generated images and videos. Subscribers can now upload clips for analysis, with the software providing a heatmap to flag AI-manipulated sections and distinguishing between partially and entirely synthetic content.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/pangram-expands-ai-detection-to-images-and-video.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Additionally, the company updated its image detection model, now accessible via a Chrome extension that scans browsing content. While the tool boasts high accuracy in testing, the company notes that images below 512 x 512 resolution receive reduced confidence scores. CEO Max Spero stated the model was specifically calibrated to differentiate between AI-generated visuals and standard AI post-processing used by modern smartphone cameras.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The brief provides a clear summary of the new detection capabilities and technical limitations mentioned in the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2307116/pangram-expands-its-ai-detection-tools-to-video-and-images/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
