     <article class="story single" data-beat="science">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">SCIENCE</span> · Phys.org · 2026-11-04 · editor 9/10 · 3 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Satellite Study Finds Limited Ground Warming From Hyperscale Data Centers</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#data centers</span> <span>#climate change</span> <span>#satellite data</span> <span>#environmental impact</span></div>
       <p>A new study published in Environmental Research Letters challenges previous assumptions about the environmental impact of hyperscale data centers, finding little evidence that these facilities cause significant heating of the surrounding land. Contrary to an earlier study that suggested data centers could heat adjacent land by an average of 2 °C, with peaks up to 9 °C, extending up to 10 kilometers (6.2 miles) away, the latest research indicates a minimal effect. This comprehensive analysis, which utilized 13 years of satellite data, revealed that waste heat from servers causes only negligible nighttime surface warming, while the building materials themselves contribute to cooling ground temperatures during daylight hours.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-11-04/satellite-study-finds-limited-ground-warming-from-hyperscale.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The study employed energy balance calculations, which demonstrated that generating the previously suggested large regional temperature increases would necessitate 6 to 43 times more heat than the facilities are capable of releasing. To reach these conclusions, researchers tracked 13 years of thermal satellite imagery across 12 data centers constructed between 2011 and 2025 within Japan's largest data center hub near Tokyo. By comparing operating facilities against control sites and analyzing daytime versus nighttime readings, the study found that commissioning a data center actually cools local ground temperatures by 1.8°C to 2.2 °C during the day. This cooling effect is attributed to modern engineered roofs reflecting more sunlight than the cropland or open fields they replace. At night, when the exhaust heat from servers would be most apparent, ground temperature changes directly over the facilities remained negligible, with multikilometer cluster nighttime ground warming staying below 0.3 °C.</p><p>Cross-climate tests conducted in dry and continental environments reinforced these findings, confirming that any observed nighttime surface warming occurred during the construction phase, prior to servers being installed or powered on. This warming pattern matched that of regular commercial developments, such as warehouses. While slight air warming, typically under 1 °C, might occur within a few hundred meters of concentrated cooling vents in dry climates, the study suggests that server heat is not potent enough to induce widespread ground warming across neighborhoods. Currently, no major data center market mandates temperature impact assessments before construction, although some U.S. local zoning guidance has begun to address heat without specifying measurement methods.</p><p>The research establishes an open, satellite-based testing tool that city planners and regulators globally can utilize to accurately measure local thermal impacts both before and after facilities are built. Lead author Jiawei Jing from the Makuhari Development Corporation emphasized the importance of this evidence for communities and planners. Jing stated, &quot;Communities and planners need evidence when deciding where and how data centers should be built. As governments invest in the digital infrastructure needed to support AI and economic growth, it's important that decisions are guided by what satellite imagery and energy balances say, to ensure environmental impacts are measured accurately and transparently.&quot; The study, titled &quot;Satellite observations and energy balance constrain the thermal footprint of a hyperscale data-centre cluster,&quot; was authored by Jiawei Jing and published in Environmental Research Letters in 2026.</p><p>What to watch: Adoption of the new satellite-based testing tool by city planners.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Captures the study's quantitative findings, methodology, and quotes cleanly.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://phys.org/news/2026-11-satellite-analysis-evidence-centers-widespread.html" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
